\f2 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f3\fs26 The band, simply called \'91Elvis\'92, arrived early knowing the security checks required to get to the top floor of Hardy Tower 1 would take an age. Suspiciously, but not surprisingly, they were ushered through security and were up on the top floor in no time. The Gravity Well Lounge, a pick-up joint for the ultra rich and desperate, was located directly adjacent to the restaurant of the same name. All ready to fight to the death, they didn\'92t make it three paces off the elevators before all were silently taken out by darts and surreptitiously spirited away. \
 A well groomed, bi-spectacled waiter, head adorned with a brand new and very expensive wig, caught the well practiced moves of the agents in the corner of his eye, quickly re-entered Gravity Well, heading for the tables at the rear. In his haste he bumped a table, causing a large glass of Shiraz to teeter and threaten to spill the contents, but it was snatched out of the air by the diner who, without pause, gracefully brought glass to her lips and took a sip.\
\'93A lovely vintage.\'94 Sarah said.\
\'93They have already been taken, without so much as a shot fired.\'94 Cracker blurted out, not caring that Shakti was on the other side of the small table and could hear everything. \'93The wheels are already in motion.\'94\
\'93Can I help you?\'94 Shakti inquired. \'93I think you have the wrong table.\'94 \
\'93It\'92s me Alex.\'94 Cracker took off his glasses, but his was insufficient for Shakti to make the connection, requiring him to take off his new wig. \
\'93Cracker, is that really you?\'94 Shakti always had to ask to stupid questions. \'93Oh, that\'92s right you are alive again and everything forgiven.\'94\
\'93Is there are problem here?\'94 The head waiter had seen the fuss and wanted to minimise the discomfort to other diners. \
\'93No. There is no problem, I told the the waiter I loved the wine, but my husband\'85\'94 She coughed daintily. \'93\'85 disagreed with me and I sought a third opinion. He was about to give that opinion when you interrupted. I would like to hear it, if you do not mind or would you rather I call over the maitre \'92d.\'94\
\'93Of course not, madam.\'94 Peering over the top of his glasses he gazed upon Cracker with suspicion. \'93I will leave you to it.\'94\
\'93Alex, do you remember when I said I was going to ask you for a favour? A favour that you had to grant no questions asked?\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94 \
\'93I need to borrow Sarah for the night. No shenanigans I promise, I just needs her to look at something.\'94 He was sure that sounded better in his head. \
\'93Can\'92t we finish dinner first?\'94 Shakti was having a very pleasant evening and he was looking forward to the crab. \'93It takes the lab years to grow my main course and I do not want it to go to waste.\'94\
\'93That isn\'92t exactly no questions asked, but if you insist. As soon as you are finished.\'94 He looked over his shoulder and saw the head-waiter, standing at the bar, arms crossed with one foot ferociously tapping the floor. \'93I think I have outstayed my welcome here.\'94 Cracker turned and walked toward the bar leaving the Shakti\'92s to their homogenized, bio-tech crab-like-things.\
\'93What the fuck do you think you are doing, Cracker?\'94 The head waiter brought Cracker in close so he could yell in his ear quietly. \'93This is Gravity Well. This is the premier restaurant in the world. This is the last time. The favour paid and we are even.\'94\
\'93Okay, tonight is the last time.\'94 Cracker thought he was telling the truth.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi720\sl360\slmult1\sa160\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f5 * * *
\f0 \
