\f2 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f3\fs26 For the next hour Cracker made sure he kept his head down and kept out of sight behind the set of giant drapes adorning the eastern internal wall. The head waiter was on the war path and would throw him out at any further disruption to the diners. When he saw the last of Shakti\'92s desert disappear he waved Sarah over. She gave Shakti a kiss on the cheek and she headed to the drapes and disappeared behind them.\
\'93This is a bit strange, Cracker.\'94 Sarah swept a few crumbs from the front of her shirt with the back of her fingers as she looked upon at the non-descript stone wall. \'93There does not appear to be anything to see here.\'94\
Cracker raised his eyebrows, \'93Is that right?\'94. He then sidled along the wall for several metres where the stone began to shimmer, a door appeared and opened inward and Cracker slipped inside. Sarah followed quickly and as the door silently closed behind her, Cracker lit up the room to reveal the long dormant operations centre. \
\'93I found it on your allocation night. I showed Kathy, but there was not enough time to work out what the hell this is.\'94\
\'93Well, the hardware in here is about 20 years out of fashion, but would have been state of the art when it was manufactured.\'94 She looked over the bevy of clunky buttons and knobs and after some consideration, powered the ancient computers up, which in turn switch on a miscellaneous array of old 2-D monitors, surveillance systems, environmental controls and strange humming machines. \'93The processing power of this system is really quite good. It is comparable to most small systems we have in place today and\'85my goodness, free lunch for everyone! The specifications say this was manufactured twenty three years ago and has a quantum processor. I would have thought that impossible. The idea of quantum processors has been around for a long time, but we are yet to perfect the technology.\'94\
\'93What\'92s this thing?\'94 Cracker opened a the door on the big white machine, an interior light revealed a white space with four empty glass shelves and empty storage compartments on the inside of the door. \'93It stinks a bit.\'94\
\'93That is a refrigerator. They were used before stasis cupboards.\'94 Sarah was taken back to her childhood and the many times she was scolded by her mother, 
\f4\i \'91Shut the door, you are letting the cold out!\'92 
\f3\i0 It course it was the heat getting in not the other way around, she had told her mother a thousand times. \'93Are we able to stay long? This is going to take a while. I should be able to access most of the systems, given enough time.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi720\sl360\slmult1\sa160\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f5 - - -
\f0 \
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\pardeftab720\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f3\fs26 The invention of the stasis cupboard was one of the greatest leaps forward in food hygiene. The technology behind the storage bins was a highly protected secret that the PTD would not allow into the public domain. Industrial spies had been regularly executed to ensure the secret remained safe. No one person possessed all of the knowledge and stasis cupboard technicians were the highest paid maintenance personnel in the world. Joining the ranks of the Stasis Technicians Association was by invitation only and akin to joining the Illuminati. An invitation to become a Stasis Technician was one of the highest honours and no-one ever refused. Theirs was a secretive society and once joined you could never leave.\
Precisely why you had to be superman to repair a fridge, was a conundrum that no-one outside the association could answer. It was also a question that no-one inside the association would answer. These people were paid obscene amounts of money to perform, what appeared to the uninitiated observer, menial tasks. While it was reasonably clear what the stasis cupboards, preserve food, it was infinitely more difficult to divine how that was achieved. Perishable items placed inside the cupboard would not deteriorate in any way, no matter how long they were in there. It was as if, on the inside of the cupboards, time had stopped.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi720\sl360\slmult1\sa160\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf0 <!$Scr_Ps::0>
\f5 - - -
\f0 \
\pard\tx360\tx720\tx1080\tx1440\tx1800\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\pardeftab720\fi360\sl264\slmult1\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf0 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f3\fs26 \'93Kathy thought this place had not been used in years, so I think we are here alone, but I\'92ll take a look around if you like.\'94 \
Cracker commenced his reconnoiter, wandering off along a brightly lit corridor to nowhere. Just a dead end and a blank wall, however, as he hoped, the stonewall shimmered and another glistening metallic door appeared and opened into a palatial and luxuriously appointed apartment. The floor gleamed throughout with white marble, complementing ostentatious fittings and furnishings of brocade silk and gilded trim. The opulent decor, heavily layered detail upon decadent design, for no other reason than an ostentatious display of wealth. Massive tapestries and full length portraits hung from the walls and from the ceiling of each room immense crystal chandeliers. His astonishment grew as he wandered from room to room, mouth agape. He counted no less that six bedrooms and seven bathrooms, a sauna, steam room, lap pool and an arboretum bulging with overgrown trees and bushes, some pressing upon the the glass walls and ceiling. \
Past the arboretum lay a fully appointed medical facility, filled with stainless steel apparatus of varying kinds, but upon the examination table was a body of a male, dessicated and flaking after decades in the dry, environmentally controlled air. The hair atop the man\'92s head had fallen onto the floor, in a pile of brittle brown strands and the examination table was stained with bodily juices that had oozed out in rivulets and then, over time, dried up into brown organic refuse. Cracker had seen plenty of bodies before and was more fascinated than disgusted by the mummified remains lying there before him. He flicked a few switches on the nearby machines, but he could not get them fire-up and decided this was a job for Sarah Shakti, pointy-headed computer-geek.\
Sarah was seated in front of an antiquated QWERTY keyboard, working furiously on a peculiar program she had stumbled across. From what she could gather, this application had been silently operating for decades, while the rest of the server remained all but dormant. No matter how she attacked the problem, she could not get around the security system that demanded DNA identification. Having hit a dead end, she decided to find Cracker, who had been gone an eternity. She she followed the only corridor to a blank stone wall, but could go not further. \
\'93Cracker!\'94 She yelled out. \'93Cracker? Hello? Where are you?\'94 The stone facade in front of her shimmered and a door opened, allowing Cracker to re-enter the corridor. \'91And I didn\'92t have to say open-sesame!\'94\
\'93You should see what\'92s in there. It must be the most expensive apartment in all of the Primary Trade Dominion.\'94 Cracker was wide eyed and excited. \'93Even you will be impressed, I think.\'94\
\'93Just a quick look.\'94 She never found inspecting real estate all that compelling, having bought most of her properties sight-unseen, but thought a few minutes away from the cyber-puzzle would not be too much of a distraction. \'93Anyway, I\'92ve hit a bit of an impasse with the security system, but given enough time, I can work it out.\'94 The entry door shimmered into existence, Cracker stood to the side, gentlemanly granting Sarah first access. Sarah had grown used to level 5 splendor, but even she had never seen sickening luxuriance and edifying magnificence blended so perfectly. \'93Well, I must say I have never seen a property where the relation between form and function blurs under the weight of layer upon layer of detail. This is not a place not of pragmatism, but beauty purely for the sake of beauty.\'94\
\'93Huh?\'94\
\'93I like it.\'94 She decided the overstated nature of the decor required an understated reaction.\
\'91Okay. Come through here to the med-lab.\'94 Cracker pointed beyond the arboretum and the lap pool to a glass doorway beyond. \'93I couldn\'92t get the machines on line.\'94\
\'93You probably should have flicked the power switch.\'94 Sarah promptly caused the machines to started beeping and buzzing. \'93That\'92s a surprise. Readouts say this is the body of Jameson Hardy and he has been dead for 20 years. Well, I guess we have solved that mystery. According to the records he died of a viral infection around the same time as the great viral cleansing.\'94\
\'93Money could not save him?\'94 \
\'93No, it never does.\'94 Looking upon the dried husk, she took a moment and then smiled. Her mind was now ablaze, complex algorithms flashing through neurons, subconsciously assessing possible solutions and rejecting all but one. She picked up a skeletal arm and scraped away a chunk of dessicated skin and dusted into a specimen jar. \'93Let\'92s go.\'94\
\'93Why do you need the dandruff?\'94 Cracker had encountered many gristly scenes cleaning up after homicides, but even he found himself a little disgusted. \
\'93I have located a system that requires DNA identification to pass security. This should suffice.\'94 She quickly shuffled out of the apartment and returned to the control room with Cracker having to play catch up. \
Sarah sat before the array of monitors and dumped the skin dust on the security scanner. The central monitor, sitting quietly before Sarah immediately announced the system was unlocked and she had access to everything. \'93Double my tax cheque! This is not to be believed.\'94 She said as she continued to scroll through record after record, jumping from vast spreadsheets to bank statements, her fears confirmed with every entry she scanned. She was unable to sit still, becoming more and more animated by the surprising data appearing before her eyes, now close to popping out of their sockets.\
\'93Well, out with it.\'94 Cracker was staring at the screen, but did not understand the significance of the data. \'93What are you so excited about?\'94\
\'93This room is the control centre for the entire empire of Jameson Hardy. Everything he did and everything he had planned is recorded in here. The system we just opened is the portal to his financial records and institutions. A few of the foreign interests appear to be hyper-portable and multi-jurisdictional, regularly changing locations of legal and quasi-legal entities.\'94\
\'93In English?\'94\
\'93Jameson Hardy\'92s foreign banking institutions, manage hidden wealth with indifference or outright defiance of the interests and laws of multiple nation states. He owned private banks for ultra rich clients specialising in tax evasion.\'94\
\'93A tax haven?\'94\
 \'93He had majority interests in three banks, but he had also set up his own investment concerns in various places around the world, but all of those institutions were controlled from here, by this system. I have only taken a small sample, but it appears every wealthy family and most corporations in the Primary Trade Dominion hid trillions in those tax havens. Most of the money was deposited about two decades ago and none of it has ever been withdrawn.\'94\
\'93Why not?\'94 Cracker wanted to withdraw some cash there and then. \
\'93My guess is Jameson Hardy was the only person with the access. When he disappeared, all access to that money was cut off. They still get vast dividends from their investment, but they can\'92t touch the principal investment. She pondered the horror of losing access so much money. \'93That must have sent shock waves through the executive classes, many heads would have rolled.\'94\
\'93Can we access the money?\'94 Cracker thought the direct approach was best.\
\'93I am not certain, but what do you have in mind?\'94 She knew she could access the money, but did not want to let Cracker dream, not yet anyway.\
\'93I would like to give a fat cheque to all of the poor.\'94\
\'93No, that is admirable, but also dangerous. The economy would react quickly to such vast amounts of money being dumped into the market at once.\'94 She saw Cracker didn\'92t seem convinced. \'93If you made everyone a millionaire one week, the price of bread would be 1000 a loaf the following week.\'94\
\'93And all of the money would end up back where it began?\'94\
\'93Exactly. The structures in place would ensure the money would be soaked back up by the rich. It would, in fact, make the wealthy, wealthier.\'94\
\'93Not the result I was looking for.\'94 Cracker scratched his head. 
\f4\i This economics stuff is too hard.
\f3\i0  \'93I will have to give it a bit of thought, but can I make a bit of a withdrawal?\'94\
\'93Not, now. I will have to get familiar with the system, first.\'94 She got back to work, fingers a blur as she tapped at the clunky keyboard. \
\'93Why do the doors open for me?\'94 \
\'93To my amazement, you must actually be related to Jameson Hardy. I think the doors are set to allow through relatives and maybe a few honoured colleagues. Your genetics must be sufficiently similar to qualify you as a relative.\'94 She did not look up from her work as she continued. \'93I doubt if you could access the finance data-base, but perhaps we can change that. He must have had some sort of succession plan in place.\'94 Sarah continued for hours.\
The passage of time, relative to the observer, was largely dependent upon the activity the time was wasted on. Cracker sat on the floor for hours before finally being drawn back to the sumptuous apartment. For Sarah, however, it only seemed like a couple of minutes before she once again raised her head and saw a gilded edged plate sitting before her containing most perfect examples of fresh fruit she had seen since since her childhood. She reached out to touch the brightly coloured temptations, but baulked, momentarily thinking it was a still-life hologram.\
\'93I found them hanging from the trees in there.\'94 Proudly announced Cracker, hunter-gatherer. \'93Can you eat them?\'94\
\'93Are you kidding? Look at this pear.\'94 She brought the light green tear-drop to her mouth and bit into the white juicy flesh with vigor. \'93Mmm, that is so good. Try that, it\'92s an orange\'94 She said as pear nectar ran to her elbows, but she did not care and dove back in, audibly slurping. \'93Peel it, first.\'94\
\'93I know.\'94 Cracker tore the sphere in two, releasing sweet juice onto the floor. He had seen oranges on the link and had keenly gulped down orange-like fruit drink (with faux-pulp added) his entire youth. He had a good idea of what to expect, but gingerly took a bite and instantly his mouth was filled with a sweet pure taste explosion of natural fruit, but it was also acidic, wonderfully so! His delight and surprise saw Sarah laugh, gratified she had introduced him to such a simple pleasure. \'93I have no words. What\'92s that?\'94\
\'93A guava, maybe.\'94 She thought that pears and guavas should not grow in the same environment, but maybe they were special varieties. \'93Watch for little seeds.\'94\
\'93That is even better.\'94 He then began to taking one bite from each offering. \'93All so good!\'94\
\'93Was that your first time?\'94\
\'93I will alway remember this moment, when I lost my virginity to a banana.\'94 He inserted the pointy end of a banana in his mouth and bit down hard. \'93Mmm. Love that creamy texture.\'94 They both had a bit of a giggle, but cut it short, unable to help themselves finishing off the plate. \'93How much money are we talking about?\'94\
\'93At least trillions, maybe tens of trillions. The system has been operating for 20 years and has been trading the whole time. From what I have seen it is programmed to spread millions of low risk, modest investments, over many markets and industries. Everything low risk. Eeverything short term. The return on each investment is small and generally assured, but the accumulation of these investments over 20 years is stratospheric.\'94 She could see that Cracker wanted a number. \'93Let\'92s put it this way. If you are entitled to Jameson Hardy\'92s investments and offshore accounts, you are the richest person that has ever lived.\'94\
\'93Cool. Who should I kill first?\'94 Cracker pulled his best poker face but, when Sarah burst out, he could not help but let out in a hearty guffaw. \
\'93There is much for thought, Emperor.\'94 Sarah bowed. \
